{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's the downspout math for a typical Scottsboro lake house:</p>

<p>2,400 square feet of roof. Four downspouts. If they're 2x3 (the builder-grade standard), each handles 600 square feet. 4 × 600 = 2,400. Looks fine on paper.</p>

<p>Now factor in reality. North Alabama gets 55 inches of rain annually — 45% above the national average. During storm season, we regularly see 4-6 inches per hour for short bursts. The capacity numbers assume moderate rainfall. Our storms aren't moderate.</p>

<p>At 5 inches per hour, those 2x3 downspouts are overwhelmed. Water backs up in the gutters, hits the roofline, overflows. It's not a gutter problem — it's a downspout bottleneck.</p>

<p>Switch to 3x4 downspouts: same four locations, now handling 1,200 square feet each. 4 × 1,200 = 4,800 square feet of capacity for a 2,400 square foot roof. That's the margin you need for real Alabama storms.</p>

<p>The older homes in <a href="/recent-projects/">downtown Scottsboro</a> have a different problem. Original gutters from the 60s and 70s often have only 2-3 downspouts on houses that need 4-5. The math never worked — it just took decades of deferred maintenance to create visible problems.</p>

<p>We see a lot of creative DIY solutions in Scottsboro. Downspouts that route into underground pipes that are clogged. Extensions that dump into flower beds three feet from the foundation. Multiple splices and redirects that nobody remembers the logic of. Usually the fix is simpler than the accumulated "solutions": right-sized downspouts in the right locations, properly extended.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: adequate capacity, proper placement, and effective extension.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity</strong> means the right size downspouts for your roof area and local rainfall. In Scottsboro, that almost always means 3x4 instead of 2x3. The cost difference is minimal — maybe $15-20 per downspout. The capacity difference is double.</p>

<p><strong>Placement</strong> means downspouts where water naturally concentrates. Corners, valleys, and low points in the gutter run. A lot of houses have downspouts wherever was convenient for the installer rather than where the water needs to go. Sometimes moving a downspout 10 feet solves a problem that extensions and underground pipes couldn't fix.</p>

<p><strong>Extension</strong> means getting water far enough from the foundation that it can't cause problems. On Scottsboro's clay soil, that's 6-10 feet minimum. On sloped lake lots where water runs back toward the house, it often means underground routing to carry water past the problem areas entirely.</p>

<p>The technical details: downspouts should connect to gutters through an outlet sized to match (3x4 downspout needs 3x4 outlet, not 2x3 stepped up). Elbows at top and bottom should be 45° or less when possible — 90° elbows restrict flow more and clog more easily. Extensions should maintain slope to the discharge point — flat or reverse-sloped extensions hold water and breed mosquitoes.</p>
{% endset %}

